dxgi: Name the program and the time of day in the frame lines.

The three half-minute lines of patch 0010 said neither which program they were of nor when.
A launcher's swap chain can draw beside the game's (Red Dead Redemption 2's does, with
Direct3D 11, while the game draws with Vulkan), so its lines were read as the game's; and they
could be put beside other logs, or a player's account of a session, only by counting lines.
Each now says both, as win32u's Vulkan lines do:

  DXGI: frame times (s2_sp64_ship.exe, 14:03:27): 89341 frames in 1440 s, median 15.0 ms, ...
  DXGI: the last 30 s (s2_sp64_ship.exe, 14:03:27): 1650 frames, 3 over 50 ms: at 3.4 s (52 ms), ...
  DXGI: a frame of the last 30 s (s2_sp64_ship.exe, 14:03:27): 4.6 command buffers, ...

The program is the executable's file name, read once; the time is local time, read once for
the three lines. "macos/steamplay record" reads these and 0010's lines alike.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR DXMT. Applied by macos/build-dxmt.sh to
github.com/athei/dxmt at 01e59e9bde030f105937f249afaac92ada01b4d7, after 0001 to 0011.
Written with an AI assistant (Claude). DXMT does not accept contributions made or
co-authored by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be sent to it as a pull
request.
